{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 0,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We knew that the James Webb Space Telescope\nwould find interesting stuff, especially about"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4440,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the mysterious early epoch of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7440,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, there are hints that the galaxies \nwe’re seeing there are brighter and more regular"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11640,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than expected given the short amount of time\nthey’d had to grow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15180,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But perhaps no one was expecting that we \nwould find a completely new type of star—one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21000,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "powered by dark matter and shining\nas bright as an entire galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24840,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which, by the way, might help us explain those\npesky giant galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35220,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In April, a paper was published in Nature\nAstronomy reporting on the observation of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39240,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "four objects - JADES-GS-z10-0 \nthrough JADES-GS-z13-0."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44880,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll call these z10 through z13 for short."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48300,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not very poetic, but space is so big we ran\nout of cool names decades ago."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52680,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Admittedly, these don’t look all that impressive."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55740,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They’re basically just dots, but that’s\ntrue of anything too far away to properly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59580,
    "dDurationMs": 480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "resolve."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60060,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So you’ll have to trust me and be impressed\nanyway."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63360,
    "dDurationMs": 960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Why?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64320,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because these are among the oldest and most\ndistant objects ever seen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67980,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Z13 and z12 actually are the current second\nand third place holders for the most distant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "objects whose distances are actually confirmed,\nwith z11 in a respectable fifth place."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80280,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They were discovered as part of the JWST Advanced\nDeep Extragalactic Survey - or “JADES”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85830,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- whose mission is to study the very first\ngalaxies that formed in our universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91080,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is only possible with JWST because of\nits incredible sensitivity to the infrafred."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97680,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It can see stars whose energetic ultraviolet\nand visible light has been stretched far into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103500,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "infrared wavelengths as it traveled to us\nthrough an expanding universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107160,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some of these things are over 30 billion light\nyears away, meaning their light has been traveling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112800,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to us for most of the age of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114900,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re looking at the cosmos as it was only\naround 400 million years after the Big Bang,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120060,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or around 3% or less of its current age."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122940,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Based on the brightness of these blobs, at\nfirst glance it seems they must be entire"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127260,
    "dDurationMs": 600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127860,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But enormous ones, at some hundreds of millions\nof times the mass of the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131880,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Such big galaxies are common enough in the\nmodern universe, but no one expected to find"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136800,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxies so big back then, when they’d had\nso little time to form."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140940,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The issue with the big early galaxies discovered\nby JWST has caused a lot of consternation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147000,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and some pretty out-there speculation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149880,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That said, let’s at least entertain the\nhypothesis that we’re looking at something"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154500,
    "dDurationMs": 1380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "truly weird here."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155880,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, these are the cosmic dark ages\nwe’re peering into - a time when the ocean"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162000,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of pristine hydrogen forged in the Big Bang "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164280,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shrouded our vision across \nmuch of the electromagnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167640,
    "dDurationMs": 360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spectrum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168000,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s a time when that same pristine hydrogen\nwas able to form stars many thousands of times"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172920,
    "dDurationMs": 1200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more massive than today."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174120,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Who knows what else might be lurking there\nat the edge of our map?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178260,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps, here be dragons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180780,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or perhaps here be dark stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183720,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s the assertion of a new paper was\npublished in the Proceedings of the National"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187680,
    "dDurationMs": 1020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Academy of Sciences."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188700,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The idea that three of these four \nobjects might be powered by dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193320,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The original idea for dark stars \nwas proposed by Katherine Freese  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197520,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and collaborators back in 2007, but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199740,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this is the first time we’ve seen \ncandidates for these objects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202920,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I should point out that the term dark star \nwas originally used by John Michell back in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207600,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1783 to describe a very early incarnation\nof the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211140,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But these new dark stars aren’t that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213780,
    "dDurationMs": 1140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These aren’t even dark."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214920,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They are super bright."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216660,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But how can dark matter, which is meant to\nnot interact with light at all, produce so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221340,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much of the stuff?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222660,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we build our own dark star, we’re going \nto need a particular type of dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228300,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example it doesn’t work if dark matter\nis made up of mini black holes or failed stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233580,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It has to be some kind of new undiscovered\nparticle, and even then there are a few rules"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238200,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the particle has to follow to be able to make\na dark star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241140,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, it has to obey the main rule of all\ndark matter: it can’t interact strongly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245820,
    "dDurationMs": 480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246300,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means one dark matter particle can’t\neasily bounce off another one without getting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251100,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "super close."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252720,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That enables dark matter to avoid collapsing\neasily under its own gravity, which is needed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257520,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to explain how it remains as a giant puffy\ncloud surrounding nearly all galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261780,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, particles in a cloud of\ngas interact strongly with each other, generating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267000,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a kind of internal friction that saps kinetic\nenergy from the gas and allows the cloud to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271440,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shrink to form stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273960,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But with very little self-interaction, dark\nmatter stays puffy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One type of strong interaction that is doubly\nruled out is the electromagnetic interaction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282840,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dark matter can’t emit or absorb photons and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286140,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we need that for dark matter to remain dark."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289740,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The second rule for making dark stars is that,\nwhile the dark matter particle can’t interact"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295980,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strongly with itself, it \ndoes have to halve at least "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298980,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some weak self-interaction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301500,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This interaction strength has to be tuned\nso that when the cloud is large it experiences"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306960,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "almost no interaction due to the particles\nbeing so far apart."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309900,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if we can get those dark matter particles\nclose enough together then they will interact."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314400,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This allows the dark matter cloud to collapse,\njust like a gas cloud, but only under certain"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320760,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extreme circumstances."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322500,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But more importantly, it allows \ndark matter to annihilate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327240,
    "dDurationMs": 1380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s the last requirement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328620,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For our dark matter particles to form dark\nstars they have to be able to annihilate each other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333480,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This could mean the particle \nis its own antiparticle,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336840,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "allowing any two particles to self-annihilate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339360,
    "dDurationMs": 900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if they get too close."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340260,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s the assumption in the recent paper."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343560,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this could also work if antimatter comes\nin particle-antiparticle pairs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348180,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The key is that the annihilation happens when\nthe particles get close enough together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352440,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now that we know what type of dark matter\nwe need, let’s assume that’s the type"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357420,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that actually exists in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359280,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This isn’t unreasonable—popular candidates\nfor dark matter, like WIMPS for example, should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364680,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fit these requirements."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366240,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we’re going to fill our early universe\nwith lots of dark matter, smeared out pretty"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371280,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "evenly across all of space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373740,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll also add some hydrogen and helium\nat about a fifth the quantity of dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378420,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And finally we have small fluctuations in\nthe density of all that matter—regions of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384480,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "higher density that would pull on the surrounding\nmaterial and so seed the first structures"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389100,
    "dDurationMs": 660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389760,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The seeds of the first giant stars would have\nbeen so-called mini-halos with masses of millions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396720,
    "dDurationMs": 3118,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to hundreds of millions of times the Sun’s\nmass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399838,
    "dDurationMs": 4622,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The dark matter part would have a hard time\ncollapsing due to being weakly interacting."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404460,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However the gas in that halo would fall towards\nthe center, perhaps en route to building a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409140,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star, depending on how large this halo was."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411540,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, as we discussed in our recent episode,\ndark matter can be strongly influenced by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416520,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the gravitational pull of regular matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418260,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s what happens here."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420300,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The growing density of gas in the center of\nthe halo would drag some of the dark matter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424320,
    "dDurationMs": 660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inwards."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424980,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The density of dark matter inside the central\nball of gas would rise to much higher levels"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430680,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than we see in the modern universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432420,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this is where our dark star is born."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435600,
    "dDurationMs": 4791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The amount of dark matter inside the star\nis relatively low—only a fraction of a percent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440391,
    "dDurationMs": 1209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the mass of the gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441600,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But its concentration is trillions of times\nhigher than the dark matter in a typical galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447360,
    "dDurationMs": 5759,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means its particles can find each \nother and remember what happens then."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453119,
    "dDurationMs": 5941,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They annihilate, releasing an enormous amount\nof energy in the process—mostly in the form"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459060,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of very fast moving decay products."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461040,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That energy release heats up the surrounding\ngas and actually stops it from contracting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466260,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any further."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467580,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The gas ball never gets dense enough for form\na true star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470760,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that doesn’t matter, because the dark\nmatter annihilation is producing far more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476100,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy than a regular star ever could."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479160,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our newborn dark star starts out as a bloated\nball a few times Earth’s orbital radius,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485100,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and several times the mass of the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487020,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But then it grows, pulling more gas and\ndark matter from the rich material of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492120,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surrounding young universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493920,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It grows in size several times, perhaps to\nthe size of Saturn’s orbit, and it may grow"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499320,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to millions of times the mass of the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501180,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At that size, it’s glowing billions of times\nbrighter than the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505800,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bright enough to be seen by JWST \nat the ends of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510360,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ok, let’s assume for a moment \nthat dark stars once existed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513840,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What happened to them?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515640,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eventually the dark matter would annihilate\nenough of itself that it could no longer support"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520740,
    "dDurationMs": 2160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the gas cloud from collapsing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522900,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However it’s also possible that a dark star\ncould replenish its supply of fuel by pulling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527700,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in dark matter from the surrounding halo."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529320,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Either way, when it’s done - be it millions\nor even billions of years, once the gas is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535140,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no longer supported it would probably collapse\npretty quickly into a black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539220,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is actually a nice \nfeature of this hypothesis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541800,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know there are giant black holes in the\ncenters of most galaxies, and those black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546540,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes seem to have grown very quickly in the\nearly universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549180,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps dark stars give us a way to produce\nthe seed black holes with a million Suns worth"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555000,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of mass that could then grow into the billion\nsolar mass monsters that we see in the very"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560580,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first quasars shining out of the early universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563400,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So are dark stars one of those speculations\nthat will be almost impossible to verify or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568740,
    "dDurationMs": 1020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "refute?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569760,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fortunately not."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571080,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are some pretty straightforward observations \nthat could distinguish a dark star from a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575760,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "massive early galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577380,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can do that from the signature wavelengths\nof light that are absorbed or emitted by gas"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582960,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in whatever these objects are."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585420,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When we split the light of a star into component\nwavelengths—what we call a spectrum—we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590700,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see that there are chunks taken out of the\nspectrum at specific wavelengths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594060,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are absorption line, and happen because\nthe bright light from deep in the star gets"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598800,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "absorbed at very particular wavelengths by\natoms near the surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602340,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, a galaxy spectrum tends\nto show what we call emission lines."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607260,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is light from cold gas in between the\nstars that’s been illuminated by the surrounding"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613320,
    "dDurationMs": 480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "starlight."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613800,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It gives us the colours you might be familiar\nwith in the beautiful images of nebulae."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620520,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now if we can get high quality spectra of these \nobjects, the presence of particular emission"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625800,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lines indicates a galaxy, but if there are\nno emission lines and we see absorption lines"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630540,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that would suggest a single star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633060,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And with that crazy luminosity, the dark star\nwould be a strong contender for our explanation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639300,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course, it could be a mix — a small galaxy\nwith a dark star in its center—but hopefully"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644160,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the spectra will help identify that case too."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646860,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course those distant blobs might just be\ngalaxies—albeit very weird ones."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 652740,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For a very clear take on these supposedly\ngiant galaxies, as well as a take-down of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657300,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some of the hype, check out Dr. Becky’s\nepisodes on the subject."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661320,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so what do we have?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662760,
    "dDurationMs": 1620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dark stars or galaxies?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664380,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, galaxies seem more likely if only because\nwe know galaxies exist, but dark stars are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670140,
    "dDurationMs": 840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "still so speculative."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670980,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this is an exciting case where we have\na new and kind of strange observation and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676920,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are clear observations we can make that’ll\nteach us more."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680760,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Whether or not these things turn out to be\ndark stars, we’re guaranteed to learn more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about that mysterious epoch when the very first \nstars - dark or light - lit up at the dawn"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691320,
    "dDurationMs": 1200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692520,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before you go we are excited to \ntell you that PBS has a new slate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696660,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of science shows coming to PBS Terra. \nOne new exciting one is HUMAN FOOTPRINT,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702180,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which biologist Shane Campbell-Staton \nexplores how the natural world is adapting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707220,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to us. The first episode explores how we humans \nare changing the course of elephant evolution  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713220,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in some very unexpected ways. So if you’d \nenjoy a unique scientific understanding of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719460,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how the world is changing or if you just like \nelephants, there’s a link in the description.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724500,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Also consider subscribing to Terra if you want \nexplore a variety of unique science shows."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730020,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re doing comment responses for the \nlast three episodes. We have the one about the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735360,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possibility that the speed of light is variable. \nThen the one about the recent detection of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740700,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational wave background with the pulsar \ntiming array. And finally the one were we asked  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745860,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whether the standard model that astrophysicists \nuse for dark matter needs to be revised.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752040,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Starting with the variable speed of light though."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755100,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Spindash64 asks whether a rapid decrease \nin the speed of light over time would be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760080,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same thing as a dramatic increase in \ndistance. So it is possible to model the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766080,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "apparent expansion of the universe as a slowing \nof the speed of light between the galaxies,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770340,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and people have tried. In fact Robert Dicke \ntried to model this and in fact all gravity in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776880,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "terms of a variable speed of light. But this \napproach doesn’t explain all of the effects  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781800,
    "dDurationMs": 8340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of general relativity—the warpable, stretchable \nspacetime fabric does a much more consistent job."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790140,
    "dDurationMs": 5982,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Will Dye asks what if the one-way \n\"speed\" of causality changes wildly,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796122,
    "dDurationMs": 5298,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "choosing all possible speeds just as \na particle chooses all possible paths?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801420,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well will this what-if is a reasonable statement \nof the Feynman path integral approach to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808140,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "calculating the trajectories of quantum \nparticles. And yeah, in this picture even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814200,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light travels many paths with many speeds \nbetween points, but when you sum these up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820380,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they cancel out leaving only the most probable \npaths—-which are those close to a straight  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824880,
    "dDurationMs": 5998,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light and traveling at close to light speed—or \nwithin the Heisenberg uncertainty limit of such."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830878,
    "dDurationMs": 6182,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay, onto the gravitational wave background. Sudoboat asks whether the Pulsar Timing Array data is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837060,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "public. The answer is yes. Google Nanograv data \nrelease and you’ll find a download link for the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843600,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "12.5 year data release and a link to the paper \ndescribing the release so you know what to do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849300,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with it. But seriously guys unless you’re really planning to do something with it, please don’t spam their server too much."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856080,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "AmblesJambles asks whether gravitational waves \ncan be lensed? For example around supermassive  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862920,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes right before they merge. The \nanswer is absolutely gravitational waves  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868140,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are subject to gravitational lensing. Grav \nlensing results from changing the shape of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874080,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the fabric of spacetime. Gravitational waves \nare waves in that same fabric. They have to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878700,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "travel along whatever weird shape spacetime finds \nitself in. That means gravitational waves do get  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884640,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "focused by large masses such as galaxies. In \nfact, some researchers have wondered whether  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890340,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the very large apparent masses observed \nin LIGO black hole mergers are actually a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896040,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "result of lensing of those waves increasing their \nintensity, which makes them look like they come  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901680,
    "dDurationMs": 6549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from bigger mergers. This isn’t the generally \naccepted story, however it’s also not impossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908229,
    "dDurationMs": 5631,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As for whether a merging supermassive black \nhole lenses its own gravitational waves.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913860,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well sort of. Before the merger, the waves emerged \nfrom very near the two black holes will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919440,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "encounter complex gravitational field, and that \ninfluences the shape of the emerging gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924660,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "waves. But this isn’t the same classic focusing, \nmagnifying effect of a typical gravitational lens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929820,
    "dDurationMs": 7380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a related question, Naimah92 asks if \ngravitational permeability is a thing, and if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937200,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so are gravitational waves subject to refraction. \nThat’s a great question - this is basically asking  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 942600,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whether gravitational waves always travel at \nthe same speed - lightspeed - through all space.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 947700,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The way to slow a gravitational wave would be \nthe same way we slow light - pass it through a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952320,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "medium in which the waves are continuously \nabsorbed and then reemitted in a coherent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957000,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way. With light that means passing it through \na medium full of electric charge. The charge  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961980,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of gravity is mass, so it’s conceivable \nthat a “medium” with enormous mass charges  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 968280,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could slow a wave - I dunno, a cloud of black \nholes”? I’ll have to think harder about this,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973260,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but I did find at least one paper that \ntalks about reflecting millimeter-scale  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977700,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational waves using a superconducting \nsheet, which supposedly works because extreme  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982800,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass movement is possible for electron pairs \ndue to the absence of friction. This would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 988500,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "obviously be very handy if you want to check \nyourself out in a gravitational wave mirror."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 993540,
    "dDurationMs": 9180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay, finally onto the episode about dark matter. inujosha asks: given that only usually cold gas can form stars,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1002720,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and since dark matter is cold, could a dark \nmatter stars theoretically exist? The answer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008600,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is yes - they’re called dark stars, and as chance \nwould have it we just told you all about them.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1013340,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But to recap, normally dark matter can’t form \nstars because it doesn’t interact strongly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020060,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough to shed the orbital angular moment of \nits particles. That’s needed for those orbits to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1026420,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shrink down and reach the high densities needed \nto produce energy. Without that interaction,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1031940,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark matter particles can be cold but still \nstay in their giant slow-moving orbits."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1038120,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "@a3d4e asks what if dark matter doesn't exist, \nand the current gravitational model just needs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1044720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be revised at scale? It’s such a great question \nthat many smart scientists have investigated the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1051200,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possibility, and are still doing so. But it’s \nseeming more and more likely that dark matter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1055640,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really is some time of stuff out there, rather \nthan just gravity behaving differently than we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1060020,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expect. One big reason for that is that we see \nthat dark matter can sometimes be separated from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1065540,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light matter, like the the bullet cluster or \nmore recent discoveries of galaxies with very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1069860,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "little dark matter. If dark matter was just \ngravity of regular matter behaving weird,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1074900,
    "dDurationMs": 6794,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then we’d at least expect it to always be \nfound where the regular matter is found and that's not the case. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1081694,
    "dDurationMs": 7369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "i.e_Above11D says if you listen carefully you \ncan tell that I have been replaced by an AI.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1089063,
    "dDurationMs": 4532,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well I object to the object to the idea that I've been replaced. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1093595,
    "dDurationMs": 3025,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That implies I was ever meat-based. The evidence to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1096620,
    "dDurationMs": 4409,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "contrary is abundant. For one thing, how do you \nthink I survive out here in space for so long?"
    } ]
  } ]
}
